#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Four gates, all blocking:
#   1. trivy                 — dependency advisories, HIGH+, production tree only
#   2. migrations + coverage — every migration replays against a real MongoDB and the
#      run is gated at 100% on every metric (yarn test:cov)
#   3. mutation              — Stryker, gated at 100 (yarn test:mutation)
#   4. Qodana                — inspections, SAST, license audit, coverage (qodana.yaml)
#
# One omission left, down from three:
#
#   lint — there is no eslint.config.js and no .prettierrc here, the one exception
#     on the platform. The content is applied migrations, and those are immutable:
#     a formatter that rewrites a file nobody may edit produces a diff nobody may
#     take. Qodana still inspects them (gate 4) — inspecting is not rewriting.
#
# ⚠️ Gate 3 is new, and this header used to argue at length that it could not exist:
# "there is no stryker.config.mjs and no test:mutation script. Mutating a migration means
# mutating an immutable, already-applied file; the answer to a survivor there is never
# 'edit the migration'." Both halves were wrong. There is a stryker.config.mjs now, and the
# immutability argument confused the subject of the edit: a surviving mutant in a migration
# says the TEST SUITE does not notice that migration being wrong, and the fix is an
# assertion in test/, which nothing forbids. The first run measured 52.92% — a repo at 100%
# coverage where more than a third of the mutants nobody would have caught. Changing
# `maxLength: 150` to `151`, `unique: true` to `false`, `'2dsphere'` to `''` or a `bsonType`
# list to `[]` broke no test at all, because every assertion was partial and no test ever
# read those bytes. test/migrationCalls.test.mjs and the two frozen-shape tests in
# test/migrations.test.mjs are what closed it.
#
# ⚠️ Coverage used to be on that list, and it is not any more. The argument was that the
# only suite here drove migrations end to end, so a statement gate would mostly measure
# whether every migration made it into MIGRATION_FILES. Two unit suites landed
# (test/migrationGuards.test.mjs, test/mongoUrl.test.mjs) and took the repo to 100% on all
# four metrics by covering exactly what the replay cannot reach — the `IndexNotFound`
# re-throws, the SEED_DEMO branches, the URL assembly. `thresholds: { 100: true }` is in
# vitest.config.mjs and `testCoverageThresholds` is in qodana.yaml. Never lower either to
# get a push through; drive the uncovered branch instead.
#
# What IS worth blocking a push on is the thing this repo actually asserts: that
# every migration still replays against a real MongoDB and leaves the schema it
# claims to. That suite drops its own database, replays all migrations up, then
# reverts them down — it fails loudly when a validator, an index or an ordering
# breaks, which is exactly the class of bug that reaches production silently.
#
# Gate 4 was the newer half until gate 3 landed. `qodana.yaml` and `qodana.sh` had been sitting in this
# repo since the sweep that gave the other nine a scan, with no hook calling either
# of them — this was the last unwired Qodana on the platform. What it adds is the JS
# inspections and taint analysis over migrations/, lib/ and setup/, the license
# audit — all of which read the tree and package.json rather than the tests — and,
# since the coverage gate landed, a second reading of the same lcov the gate above
# just wrote.
#
# ⚠️ That list used to include "the SCA vulnerable-dependency check", and it does not
# any more: the inspection Qodana actually runs is `VulnerableLibrariesLocal`, an
# offline heuristic that queries no advisory feed and reports zero everywhere. Gate 1
# is what checks dependencies now — see its own header.
#
# Ahead of gate 2: node. Gates 2 and 3 shell out to yarn, and `engines.node` is a
# HARD gate under yarn classic — a mismatch exits 1 with `The engine "node" is
# incompatible with this module`, not a warning. Without the switch below, a push
# from a shell on the machine default node dies before the first gate, under
# whatever banner that gate prints, which is never what went wrong. Gates 1 and 4 do
# not need it: both run in a container and neither calls node or yarn.
#
# Enabled by `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`, which the package.json
# `prepare` script sets automatically on `yarn install`.
#
# ⚠️ This file must stay executable. Git skips a non-executable hook with only a
# hint, so the gate disappears without failing — `marketplace-dev-public-authorization`
# shipped that way until it was caught.
set -euo pipefail

cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"

# Read the requirement from package.json rather than hard-coding it, so bumping the
# engine in one place cannot leave the hook testing against a version nothing else
# uses. Same block as marketplace-common's pre-commit, which is where it was written.
# The `|| REQUIRED_NODE=''` is load-bearing under `set -euo pipefail`: with no node on PATH the
# substitution exits 127, and `-e` plus `pipefail` then kill the hook right here — silently, with
# no output at all, before the block below can print the one message that would explain it.
# Degrading to empty instead lets node_satisfies() return 1 and the real diagnostic fire.
REQUIRED_NODE="$(node -p 'require("./package.json").engines.node' 2> /dev/null | tr -d '^~>= ')" || REQUIRED_NODE=''

# Empty means node is not on PATH at all — a different failure from the wrong version, and one
# the block below cannot describe: with nothing to interpolate it prints `engines.node ^` and a
# fix line reading `nvm install  && nvm use `, neither of which names anything.
if [ -z "$REQUIRED_NODE" ]; then
	cat >&2 <<'MSG'

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PUSH BLOCKED — node not found in PATH.

engines.node could not be read from package.json, so the version the gates need
is unknown and nvm has nothing to switch to.

Fix it with:

    nvm install --lts && nvm use --lts

then push again.
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
MSG
	exit 1
fi

node_satisfies() {
	local current
	current="$(node -v 2> /dev/null | tr -d 'v')"
	[ -n "$current" ] && [ -n "$REQUIRED_NODE" ] || return 1
	# caret semantics: same major, and not older than the pin.
	[ "${current%%.*}" = "${REQUIRED_NODE%%.*}" ] || return 1
	[ "$(printf '%s\n%s\n' "$REQUIRED_NODE" "$current" | sort -V | head -n 1)" = "$REQUIRED_NODE" ]
}

if ! node_satisfies; then
	# nvm.sh trips over unbound variables, so -u comes off around the source.
	NVM_SH="${NVM_DIR:-$HOME/.nvm}/nvm.sh"
	if [ -s "$NVM_SH" ]; then
		echo "pre-push: node $(node -v 2> /dev/null) does not satisfy engines.node ^$REQUIRED_NODE — switching via nvm."
		set +u
		# shellcheck disable=SC1090
		. "$NVM_SH" > /dev/null 2>&1 || true
		nvm use "$REQUIRED_NODE" > /dev/null 2>&1 || true
		set -u
	fi
fi

if ! node_satisfies; then
	cat >&2 <<MSG

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PUSH BLOCKED — wrong node.

node $(node -v 2> /dev/null || echo '(not found)') does not satisfy engines.node ^$REQUIRED_NODE, and nvm could
not be used to switch — either it is not installed at \${NVM_DIR:-\$HOME/.nvm}/nvm.sh
or that version is not installed. yarn refuses to run at all in this state, so
the migration gate below could not report anything.

Fix it with:

    nvm install $REQUIRED_NODE && nvm use $REQUIRED_NODE

then push again.
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
MSG
	exit 1
fi

# Every gate below shells out to yarn. Without this check a missing binary is reported as
# whichever gate happened to run first ("eslint or prettier is not clean"), which sends the
# reader after the wrong thing. pre-commit already carries the same check.
if ! command -v yarn > /dev/null 2>&1; then
	cat >&2 <<'MSG'

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PUSH BLOCKED — yarn not found in PATH.

None of the gates below could run: every one of them shells out to yarn.

Fix it with:

    npm i -g yarn@1.22.22

then push again.
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
MSG
	exit 1
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gate 1 — Trivy (dependency advisories). The three gates below ask whether the
# code in this repo is correct; this one asks whether the code it installed is
# safe. Nothing else on
# the platform answered that question. Qodana's `VulnerableLibrariesLocal` is
# armed in every qodana.yaml, runs on every commit and push, and reports zero
# problems everywhere — because it is a 0.03 s offline heuristic that queries no
# advisory feed at all. `VulnerableLibrariesGlobalInspection`, the class in the
# same image that does call vulnerability-search.jetbrains.com, appears in no
# profile file and in no scan log this workspace has ever produced. A security
# check that cannot report is indistinguishable from a passing one.
#
# Not `yarn audit`: yarn 1 aborts the entire run over the unpublished
# @axiumine/marketplace-common, and `npm audit` needs a package-lock.json this
# workspace does not have. Trivy reads yarn.lock natively.
#
# ⚠️ It suppresses devDependencies by default, and that is the property that makes
# this gate keepable rather than the one everybody learns to bypass: an advisory
# in something only `yarn build` ever loads does not block a push. HIGH and
# CRITICAL only, matching qodana.yaml's own `critical: 0 / high: 0`.
#
# ⚠️ --skip-dirs .stryker-tmp is load-bearing. A crashed mutation run leaves a
# sandbox behind holding a COPY of yarn.lock as it was that day, and trivy reports
# it as a second target with the same weight as the real one. One such sandbox in
# this workspace still carried axios@0.21.4 and picomatch@2.3.1 after both had
# left the actual tree — a finding about a file nobody installs from.
#
# The vulnerability database is a 100 MB+ download shared through the named docker
# volume below, so only the first run on a machine pays for it, and it updates
# itself independently of the pinned image tag: a tree that is clean today can go
# red tomorrow with no commit in between. That is the gate working, not flaking.
#
# Bypass: `SKIP_TRIVY=1 git push` — for a Docker or network outage, never for a
# finding.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

if [ -n "${SKIP_TRIVY:-}" ]; then
	echo 'pre-push [1/4]: dependency-advisory gate skipped — SKIP_TRIVY is set.' >&2
else
	# Same contract as the node and yarn checks above: a missing prerequisite blocks,
	# it never warns and continues. A gate that steps aside when it cannot run is not
	# a gate, it is a hole one condition lower.
	trivy_block() {
		local reason="$1"
		shift
		{
			echo
			echo '────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────'
			echo "PUSH BLOCKED — $reason"
			echo
			echo 'Fix it with:'
			echo
			printf '    %s\n' "$@"
			echo
			echo 'Bypass once (every other gate still runs): SKIP_TRIVY=1 git push'
			echo '────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────'
		} >&2
		exit 1
	}

	TRIVY_IMAGE='aquasec/trivy:0.70.0'

	# An absent lockfile is not an empty one: trivy would find no language-specific
	# file, report nothing and exit 0, which reads exactly like a clean tree.
	[ -f yarn.lock ] ||
		trivy_block 'yarn.lock is missing from the repo root — the scan would have nothing to read and would pass.' \
			'git checkout yarn.lock'

	command -v docker > /dev/null 2>&1 ||
		trivy_block 'docker not found in PATH — the scan runs inside the pinned trivy container.' \
			'# install Docker Engine, then push again:' \
			'https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/'

	docker info > /dev/null 2>&1 ||
		trivy_block 'docker daemon not reachable.' \
			'sudo systemctl start docker'

	docker image inspect "$TRIVY_IMAGE" > /dev/null 2>&1 ||
		trivy_block "trivy image $TRIVY_IMAGE is not present locally." \
			"docker pull $TRIVY_IMAGE"

	echo "pre-push [1/4]: checking dependency advisories ($TRIVY_IMAGE)…"

	# --db-repository is not a preference: from here mirror.gcr.io answers 404 for
	# the database layer and trivy treats that as fatal rather than falling back to
	# its second source. The mount is read-only — the scan reads the tree and the
	# cache volume, and writes nothing into the repo.
	if ! docker run --rm \
		-v "$PWD":/src:ro \
		-v trivy-db-cache:/root/.cache/trivy \
		"$TRIVY_IMAGE" fs \
		--db-repository ghcr.io/aquasecurity/trivy-db:2 \
		--scanners vuln \
		--skip-dirs node_modules \
		--skip-dirs .stryker-tmp \
		--skip-dirs .qodana \
		--severity HIGH,CRITICAL \
		--exit-code 1 \
		--skip-version-check \
		/src; then
		cat >&2 <<'MSG'

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PUSH BLOCKED — a HIGH or CRITICAL advisory in the production tree, or the scan
could not run. Both exit 1; the output above says which.

Every finding is printed with its CVE id, the version installed and the version
that fixes it. In order:

  1. Upgrade the dependency. If it is transitive, `yarn why <name>` names the one
     that pulls it in, and that is the one to move.
  2. If the package is only ever loaded by the toolchain, it belongs in
     devDependencies — moving it there is the fix, and the scan stops seeing it.
  3. Only when there is no upgrade path: a `.trivyignore` entry, one line per CVE
     id, with a comment naming the risk-register row that accepted it. Reviewed,
     per finding, dated. Never a --severity edit, never a blanket ignore.
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
MSG
		exit 1
	fi

	echo 'pre-push [1/4]: no HIGH or CRITICAL advisory in the production tree.'
fi

echo "pre-push [2/4]: replaying every migration against a real MongoDB, gated at 100% coverage (yarn test:cov)…"

if ! yarn test:cov; then
	cat >&2 <<'MSG'

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PUSH BLOCKED — the suite is red, or coverage fell below 100%.

Coverage first, because it is the newer half of this gate: vitest.config.mjs
carries `thresholds: { 100: true }`, so a statement, branch, function or line
that no test reaches fails the push with a threshold message rather than an
assertion. The fix is the missing test, never a lower number. What the replay
cannot reach by construction — the `IndexNotFound` re-throws, the SEED_DEMO
branches, lib/mongoUrl.js — is covered by test/migrationGuards.test.mjs and
test/mongoUrl.test.mjs; a new migration with a guarded dropIndex needs a row in
the former's `guarded` table.

The rest is the migration suite itself. It is not a unit suite: it drops its own
database, runs all migrations up, asserts the resulting
collections/validators/indexes, then runs them back down. A failure means one of:

  1. A migration is genuinely broken — a validator, an index, or the order two
     of them run in. Fix it in a NEW migration; applied migrations are immutable
     and are never edited (see CLAUDE.md, *Migrations are immutable*).
  2. A new migration was added but not registered in the suite's MIGRATION_FILES
     list, so the replay and the assertions disagree about what should exist.
  3. MongoDB is not reachable, or the MONGO_TEST_* block in .env is incomplete.
     The connection is assembled from MONGO_TEST_CONN_STRING + MONGO_TEST_UDBOWNER
     + MONGO_TEST_PWDDBOWNER + MONGO_TEST_AUTH_ADMIN — a populated block does
     nothing on its own unless lib/mongoUrl.js assembles it. See CLAUDE.md,
     *Testing*, for the full precedence table.

Re-run `yarn test:cov` until it is green, then push again.
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
MSG
	exit 1
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gate 3 — mutation. Coverage asks whether a line RAN; mutation asks whether a
# test would have FAILED had that line been wrong, and in this repo the two
# answers diverged by half: 100% coverage, 52.92% mutation score.
#
# It is deliberately here and not in pre-commit. The run takes ~9 minutes at
# concurrency 1, and the concurrency is 1 because all three suites share one real
# MongoDB database that the migration suite drops in its own beforeAll — two
# workers are two processes wiping each other's collections mid-replay, and the
# resulting failures get RECORDED AS KILLS, so overload reads as a high score
# rather than as a broken run. stryker.config.mjs carries the long form, including
# why a per-worker database cannot be built here (the two test users are scoped
# `dbOwner` on that one database).
#
# ⚠️ No SKIP_TESTS-style reuse is possible: Stryker runs the suite once per mutant
# under its own instrumentation, so nothing gate 2 produced can stand in for it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

echo 'pre-push [3/4]: mutation testing, gated at 100 (yarn test:mutation) — ~9 minutes, concurrency 1…'

if ! yarn test:mutation; then
	cat >&2 <<'MSG'

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PUSH BLOCKED — the mutation score is below 100, or Stryker failed to run.

`thresholds.break: 100` in stryker.config.mjs is what fails this. Never lower
it; the survivor is a real gap in the suite. Open reports/mutation/mutation.html
— every survivor is shown as the exact diff Stryker applied, which is the change
no test noticed.

Three things a survivor here usually means, in order of likelihood:

  1. An assertion is PARTIAL. This was the whole story on the first run: the
     suite checked that an index EXISTED by name and that a validator had the
     right title, so every literal inside either was unasserted. The frozen-shape
     tests are the answer — test/migrationCalls.test.mjs freezes the ordered
     driver-call log of every migration's up() and down(), and the two "is frozen"
     tests in test/migrations.test.mjs freeze the resulting validators and index
     sets. A new migration is picked up by both automatically; run `yarn test -u`
     and READ THE DIFF before committing the new snapshot.

  2. The mutated line is in a branch no suite enters. Copy
     test/migrationGuards.test.mjs: it drives the failure paths a working MongoDB
     never produces, with a fake `db`.

  3. The mutant is genuinely EQUIVALENT — no input can tell the two versions
     apart. The fix is to change the CODE so the condition can be falsified, or
     to delete it if it can never be, with the argument written at the site. A
     `// Stryker disable next-line <Mutator>: <reason>` is the last resort and
     needs the reason. Never `ignoreStatic` — it masks real gaps platform-wide.

⚠️ A migration being immutable does NOT make its survivors unfixable. The subject
of the fix is the test suite, not the migration: a survivor says the suite would
not notice that migration being wrong.

If the run died rather than scored, check MongoDB is reachable — Stryker replays
the real suite against the real database, once per mutant.
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
MSG
	exit 1
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gate 4 — Qodana. It runs here as well as in pre-commit, for two reasons that
# pre-commit structurally cannot cover.
#
# 1. `git merge --no-ff` never fires pre-commit. Git runs that hook for
#    `git commit` only, so in the branch → commit → merge → push flow the merge
#    commit — the one revision that actually reaches origin — is the single
#    commit no pre-commit scan ever sees. Two individually clean branches can
#    merge into a tree that is not.
# 2. Qodana Cloud files every report under the branch it was produced on, and
#    pre-commit always runs on the feature branch, before the commit exists. A
#    repo gated only there can never produce a `main`-tagged report, so `main`
#    is not offered as the cloud project's default branch and the "new problems"
#    baseline has nothing stable to compare against. This hook runs after the
#    merge, standing on main, which is exactly the revision the baseline wants.
#
# SKIP_TESTS=1, exactly as the other thirteen repos pass it: qodana.sh would
# otherwise re-run `yarn test:cov` to produce coverage/lcov.info, and gate 2 above
# has just written it. Worth being precise about why the flag matters and not only
# that it saves a replay — qodana.sh runs the suite as `yarn test:cov || true`, so
# a regenerated report comes with its exit code swallowed. Reusing the one the
# blocking gate produced keeps the threshold failure loud.
#
# Bypass: `SKIP_QODANA=1 git push` (the migration and mutation gates still run).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

if [ -n "${SKIP_QODANA:-}" ]; then
	echo 'pre-push: Qodana gate skipped — SKIP_QODANA is set.' >&2
	exit 0
fi

# $1 = what is wrong, $2… = the command(s) that fix it. A missing prerequisite
# blocks; it never warns and continues. A gate that steps aside when it cannot
# run is not a gate, it is the hole this hook exists to close, reopened one
# condition lower.
push_block() {
	local reason="$1"
	shift
	{
		echo
		echo '────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────'
		echo "PUSH BLOCKED — $reason"
		echo
		echo 'Fix it with:'
		echo
		printf '    %s\n' "$@"
		echo
		echo 'Bypass once (the migration and mutation gates still run): SKIP_QODANA=1 git push'
		echo '────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────'
	} >&2
	exit 1
}

command -v qodana > /dev/null 2>&1 ||
	push_block 'the qodana CLI is not in PATH.' \
		'# install it, then push again:' \
		'https://github.com/JetBrains/qodana-cli#installation'

command -v docker > /dev/null 2>&1 ||
	push_block 'docker not found in PATH — the CLI runs the linter in a container.' \
		'# install Docker Engine, then push again:' \
		'https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/'

docker info > /dev/null 2>&1 ||
	push_block 'docker daemon not reachable.' \
		'sudo systemctl start docker'

for required in qodana.yaml qodana.sh; do
	[ -f "$required" ] ||
		push_block "$required missing from the repo root." \
			"git checkout $required"
done

# Track the image declared in qodana.yaml rather than hard-coding a tag here, so
# bumping the version in one place cannot leave the hook scanning with an older
# linter than the manual runs use.
LINTER="$(grep -oE 'jetbrains/qodana-js:[0-9][0-9.]*' qodana.yaml | head -n 1 || true)"

[ -n "$LINTER" ] ||
	push_block "no 'jetbrains/qodana-js:<version>' image line found in qodana.yaml." \
		'# restore the `image:` key, then push again:' \
		'git checkout qodana.yaml'

# The CLI would pull this itself. Blocking instead keeps a slow or unreachable
# Docker Hub from turning into a failed push: one explicit pull per image bump,
# versus a network flake on any push.
docker image inspect "$LINTER" > /dev/null 2>&1 ||
	push_block "linter image $LINTER not present locally." \
		"docker pull $LINTER"

# Metadata check only — the value is never read here. qodana.sh loads the token
# into the process env without printing it.
{ [ -f ".env" ] && grep -q '^[[:space:]]*QODANA_TOKEN=' ".env"; } ||
	push_block "no QODANA_TOKEN in .env — Qodana 2023.2+ refuses to start without it." \
		'# get a project token from https://qodana.cloud, then:' \
		"echo 'QODANA_TOKEN=<token>' >> .env"

echo "pre-push [4/4]: enforcing the Qodana gate ($LINTER)…"

if ! SKIP_TESTS=1 ./qodana.sh --results-dir .qodana/results; then
	cat >&2 <<'MSG'

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PUSH BLOCKED — Qodana failed.

The gate lives in qodana.yaml: severityThresholds critical 0 / high 0,
testCoverageThresholds 100 total / 100 fresh, plus the third-party license audit
(its vulnerable-dependency inspection is the offline heuristic measured at
zero — the real check is the trivy gate above). Findings are listed above, with a report
link on qodana.cloud.

Local SARIF: .qodana/results/qodana.sarif.json

A finding in migrations/ is the awkward case, because an applied migration is
immutable: fix it in a NEW migration, or — if the inspection is simply wrong
about a file that must not change — scope an `exclude` entry to that path in
qodana.yaml with the reason written next to it. Never widen the exclude to the
whole directory.

This exit code also covers infrastructure failures (daemon died mid-scan, out
of disk). If the report shows no findings, it was not a finding.

Bypass once (the migration and mutation gates still run): SKIP_QODANA=1 git push
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
MSG
	exit 1
fi

echo 'pre-push: Qodana clean. Pushing.'
